text:
Like the sweet-apple that reddens on the topmost bough,
top on the topmost, and the apple-pickers have forgotten it;
no, they have not forgotten it, but they could not reach it.

This wave again, than the former one †. . .†
comes on, and it will give us much toil
to bale out, when it enters the ship
] . . . let us . . . [
] . . [. .] . [
[                              ]
Let us shore up [the sides] as quickly as we can,
and into a secure harbour let us run;
and let no soft hesitation [any of us]
seize; for clear before us is a great [ordeal];
remember the former [toil];
now let every man show himself steadfast,
and let us not disgrace [by cowardice]
our noble fathers who lie beneath the earth;
. . .

Come to me, leaving the island of Pelops,
mighty sons of Zeus and Leda,
with kindly heart appear, Castor
and Polydeuces;
you who over the broad earth and all the sea
go upon swift-footed horses,
and easily men from death you rescue,
from chilling death,
leaping onto the tops of well-benched ships
from afar, bright, running up the forestays,
and in the grievous night bringing light
to the black ship;
. . .

I cannot understand the strife of the winds;
for one wave rolls from this side,
another from that, and we in the middle
are carried along with the black ship,
labouring greatly in the great storm;
for the bilge-water holds the mast-step,
and the sail is all torn through already,
and there are great rents down it,
and the sheets are slackening, and the rudders [
. . .

One must not surrender one’s heart to troubles;
for we shall make no progress by grieving,
Bycchis; the best of remedies
is to bring wine and get drunk.

Let us drink; why do we wait for the lamps? There is a finger of daylight left.
Take down the big cups, †. . .† the painted ones;
for wine, the son of Semele and Zeus, as a forgetter of cares,
gave to men. Pour it in, mixing one and two,
full to the brim, and let one cup the other
push along . . .

Cleobulus I love,
for Cleobulus I am mad,
at Cleobulus I gaze.

Thracian filly, why, looking askance at me with your eyes,
do you pitilessly flee, and think I know nothing skilful?
Know well: I could neatly put the bit on you,
and holding the reins turn you round the turning-posts of the course;
but now you graze the meadows and play, skipping lightly;
for you have no skilled horse-breaking rider to mount you.
